    subDevelopmentAreas: ['Circle'],
    description: 'What is it?\n• The children explore the relationship between the diameter of a circle and its circumference.\n• They roll a metal inset circle along its edge in order to measure out its circumference as a straight line, then measure the line in diameters of the circle, ultimately discovering that the ratio of the circumference to the diameter is approximately 3.14, i.e. pi.\nWhy do we do it?\n• For children to understand that pi relates to every circle.\n• For the children to derive the formula for a calculation of circumferences.\n• For the children to use the formula to calculate the circumferences of circles.\nSupporting interest at home:\n• There are many books for children about geometric concepts.\n• You child may enjoy repeating this activity with circles in the environment.',
