    ageBand: 'Elementary (6-12)',
    developmentAreas: ['Geometry'],
    subDevelopmentAreas: ['Theorems'],
    description: 'What is it?\n• Children apply their knowledge of the Pythagorean Theorem to discover that one need not build squares on the sides and hypotenuse of a right triangle. Any similar figures built on the two sides of a right triangle will be equivalent to the similar figure built on the hypotenuse.\nWhy do we do it?\n• To apply the Pythagorean Theorem to plane figures other than squares.\n• Repetition and elaboration of Pythagorean Theorem.\nSupporting interest at home:\n• There are many books for children about geometric concepts.\n• Read about mathematicians and geometers.\n• Your child may enjoy building right-angle triangles and their squares at home, such as with construction paper.',
