    ageBand: 'Elementary (6-12)',
    developmentAreas: ['Geometry'],
    subDevelopmentAreas: ['Theorems'],
    description: 'What is it?\n• Children use a specialized metal inset plate to discover sensorially that the squares built on two legs of a right triangle are equivalent to the square built on the hypotenuse.\n• This lesson uses an isosceles right triangle, however this fact is not mentioned to the children.\nWhy do we do it?\n• To provide a sensorial impression of the Pythagorean theorem.\n• As preparation for further study.\n• To connect the areas of Geometry and History.\nSupporting interest at home:\n• There are many books for children about geometric concepts.\n• Read about mathematicians and geometers.\n• Your child may enjoy building right-angle triangles and their squares at home, such as with construction paper.',
