    description: 'What is it?\n• Children use a specialized metal inset plate to further explore the Pythagorean Theorem, in this case discovering arithmetically that the squares built on the sides of a right triangle add up to the same area as the square built on the hypotenuse.\n• This presentation uses the classic 3-4-5 Pythagorean triple, but the children are invited to explore other triangles.\nWhy do we do it?\n• To explore the numerical relationships in the Pythagorean Theorem.\n• To prepare for learning the Pythagorean Theorem in its full algebraic form.\n• Repetition and elaboration of Pythagorean Theorem.\nSupporting interest at home:\n• There are many books for children about geometric concepts.\n• Read about mathematicians and geometers.\n• Your child may enjoy building right-angle triangles and their squares at home, such as with construction paper.',
