    description: 'What is it?\n• The river model is the major material introducing the work of rivers. It can be made in a number of different ways, but usually consists of a wooden frame with a river bed and features (i.e. waterfall, natural bridge, meander, etc.) made in clay and covered with sand.\n• The children pour water at the source of the river and slowly see the features uncovered.\n• This can also be done by observing a real river.\nWhy do we do it?\n• To introduce the children to the work of rivers: carving, carrying, and depositing.\n• To introduce children to nomenclature for the parts of a river.\n• To give the children a sensorial experience of a river in order to inspire further work.\nSupporting interest at home:\n• Spend time in natural places.\n• Observe rivers at various times of the year.',
